          "session_id": "full-mt-m-007",
          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I was told my thyroid is underactive.",
          "answer": "An underactive thyroid, also known as hypothyroidism, occurs when the thyroid gland does not produce enough thyroid hormone. This hormone is crucial for setting the metabolic rate of various tissues in the body. When the thyroid is not functioning properly, the pituitary gland releases more thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) to try to stimulate the thyroid gland. This relationship is one reason why TSH levels are often the first test checked.\n\nHypothyroidism can be classified mainly into two types:\n1. **Primary hypothyroidism**: This is the most common type, where the thyroid gland itself is failing.\n2. **Central hypothyroidism**: This is less common and occurs due to issues with the pituitary gland or hypothalamus, which affect hormone production.\n\nThis information comes from the document titled \"Hypothyroidism (underactive thyroid)\" in the general clinical reference.\n\nIn terms of classification, hypothyroidism is coded under **ICD-10-CM** within Chapter 4, which covers endocrine, nutritional, and metabolic diseases. The relevant codes for disorders of the thyroid gland, including hypothyroidism, range from **E00 to E07**. Specifically, hypothyroidism falls within this range, indicating that it is categorized under endocrine disorders. This information was retrieved from the document titled \"ICD-10-CM Chapter 4: endocrine, nutritional and metabolic diseases.\"\n\nIf you have further questions about managing this condition or about specific symptoms, it's best to consult with a healthcare professional.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
